---
import Layout from '@layouts/Layout.astro';
---

<Layout title="Metro & Bus Guide for Major Chinese Cities" lang="en">
  <article class="max-w-3xl mx-auto py-12">
    <header class="mb-10">
      <div class="flex items-center gap-2 text-sm text-gray-500 mb-4">
        <a href="/en/transport/" class="text-brand-600 hover:underline">Transport</a>
        <span>&rarr;</span>
        <span>Reference</span>
      </div>
      <h1 class="text-3xl md:text-4xl font-bold text-gray-900 leading-tight mb-4">
        Metro & Bus Guide for Major Chinese Cities
      </h1>
      <p class="text-lg text-gray-500">
        How to use public transit in Beijing, Shanghai, Guangzhou, and Shenzhen. Tickets, cards, apps, and tips.
      </p>
    </header>

    <div class="prose prose-gray max-w-none">
      <p>China's major cities have world-class metro systems. They're clean, efficient, cheap, and &#8212; crucially &#8212; easy to use for English-speaking visitors.</p>

      <h2>General Tips for All Cities</h2>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Payment:</strong> You can buy single-trip tickets at machines (most accept cash and Alipay/WeChat Pay). Alternatively, use Alipay's "Transport" feature to scan QR codes directly at the turnstile.</li>
        <li><strong>Signs:</strong> Station names are in Chinese and English. Announcements are bilingual. You won't get lost.</li>
        <li><strong>Transfers:</strong> Follow the colored lines and English signs. Transfers between lines are clearly marked.</li>
        <li><strong>Apps:</strong> Apple Maps and Google Maps work for metro routing. Local apps like MetroMan or Gaode Maps (Chinese) are more detailed.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2>Beijing Metro</h2>
      <p>22 lines, 400+ stations. The network covers the entire city and extends to the suburbs. Fares start at &#165;3 and go up to &#165;9 depending on distance. The Airport Express line connects to Capital Airport (&#165;25).</p>

      <h2>Shanghai Metro</h2>
      <p>The world's largest metro system by route length. 20 lines, 508 stations. Extremely efficient. Fares: &#165;3-15. The Maglev train connects Pudong Airport to Longyang Road station in 8 minutes (&#165;50).</p>

      <h2>Guangzhou Metro</h2>
      <p>16 lines covering the city and connecting to Foshan. Fares: &#165;2-12. Clean and not as crowded as Beijing/Shanghai. The airport (Baiyun) is connected via Line 3.</p>

      <h2>Shenzhen Metro</h2>
      <p>14 lines, modern and well-maintained. The network connects to the Hong Kong, China border at Luohu and Futian stations. Fares: &#165;2-14.</p>

      <h2>Buses</h2>
      <p>Buses are cheaper than the metro (&#165;1-2) but harder to navigate as a non-Chinese speaker. Routes are numbered but stop announcements are usually in Chinese only. Google Maps or Apple Maps can help with route planning. If you're adventurous, buses can take you places the metro doesn't reach.</p>

      <h2>Transport Cards</h2>
      <p>Each city has its own transit card (Beijing: Yikatong, Shanghai: Shanghai Public Transportation Card). These can be bought at metro stations and topped up with cash. The cards also work on buses. Some cities now support the "China T-Union" standard, which allows one card to work across multiple cities.</p>

      <h2>Mobile Payment for Transit</h2>
      <p>Alipay has a "Transport" feature that generates a QR code for metro entry. Open Alipay, click "Transport" on the home screen, select your city, and scan at the turnstile. Payment is deducted from your linked card. It's the most convenient option &#8212; no need to buy tickets or carry a separate card.</p>

      <p><a href="/en/transport/transport-guide/">&larr; Back to the complete transport guide</a></p>
    </div>

    <p class="text-xs text-gray-400 mt-8">Last updated: June 11, 2026</p>
  </article>
</Layout>
